1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Welcome back.

2
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,000
On plenty of enterprise projects,

3
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,000
you can't send data to the first party API.

4
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Everything has to run inside your AWS account.

5
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
That's exactly what Amazon Bedrock solves for Claude.

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
This lesson covers how you invoke Claude on Bedrock,

7
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:21,000
how you authenticate, and which features quietly change. Let's dive in.

8
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,000
So here's the reassuring part.

9
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,000
When you move to Bedrock, your prompt logic doesn't change.

10
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Bedrock accepts the exact same messages API request body,

11
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
and it streams responses back with the same event format

12
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
you already handle.

13
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
What changes is the plumbing around that request.

14
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,000
You point at a different endpoint, you use a different client,

15
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,000
and you authenticate with AWS instead of an Anthropic key.

16
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
The current integration is called Bedrock Mantle.

17
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
And it is the path Anthropic recommends for Opus 4.7 and later.

18
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Because the body shape matches your parsing code,

19
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
your streaming consumer and your tool handling loop

20
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,000
all move over untouched.

21
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,000
You are really just swapping the front door,

22
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,000
not the room behind it.

23
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Let's look at that current path a little closer.

24
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
The Bedrock Mantle integration exposes the messages API

25
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
at a region scoped endpoint inside AWS.

26
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
So you route to your own region, rather than to Anthropic's servers.

27
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
To use it, you install the Bedrock Extra

28
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,000
for whatever language SDK you work in,

29
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,000
whether that's Python, TypeScript, or one of the others.

30
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
From there, the request you build looks identical to a first party call.

31
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
The one habit worth forming early is reading the client name.

32
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
The client you construct tells you and anyone reviewing your code

33
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,000
exactly which integration you are on.

34
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
That small signal saves a lot of confusion

35
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
when the current and legacy paths sit side by side in the same code base.

36
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,000
This is the single most important distinction to get right,

37
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,000
because both paths still work.

38
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,000
The current path is Bedrock Mantle,

39
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,000
and it pairs a bare Anthropic prefix on model IDs

40
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,000
with the Anthropic Bedrock Mantle client.

41
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,000
The legacy path is the older invoke model or converse style,

42
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
and it pairs profile prefixed identifiers,

43
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
global dot, US dot, EU dot,

44
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
and friends with the plain Anthropic Bedrock client.

45
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Here's the trap. Because the legacy path still functions,

46
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
it is easy to grab an old snippet and assume it is current.

47
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
It isn't. For Opus 4.7 and later, reach for Bedrock Mantle.

48
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Save invoke model for Opus 4.6 and earlier,

49
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
or for code you are maintaining,

50
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
rather than writing fresh.

51
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Model identifiers are where that distinction becomes concrete.

52
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
On the current Bedrock Mantle path,

53
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
the IDs carry a bare Anthropic prefix.

54
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,000
You'll write something like Anthropic dot Claude Opus 4.8,

55
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,000
or Anthropic dot Claude SONIC 5.

56
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Notice what isn't there. There is no US prefix,

57
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
no EU prefix, no regional profile wrapper.

58
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Those profile prefixed identifiers,

59
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
the ones that start with global dot,

60
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
US dot, or EU dot, belong to the legacy invoke model path,

61
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
where global is the recommended default,

62
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
and the regional forms are an opt-in at about a 10% premium.

63
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
So the rule of thumb is simple.

64
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:35,000
If your client is the Mantle client, use the bare Anthropic prefix.

65
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
If you find yourself reaching for a profile prefixed identifier,

66
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,000
you have quietly slipped back onto the legacy surface,

67
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:46,000
and you should ask whether that is really what you want.

68
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Now, authentication. Because Bedrock rides on AWS,

69
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,000
you never pass an Anthropic key.

70
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
Instead, the client resolves your AWS credentials

71
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:58,000
through a precedence order,

72
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,000
and there are three levels worth knowing.

73
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
First, anything you pass directly as constructor arguments

74
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
wins over everything else.

75
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Second, if you don't pass credentials in code,

76
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
the SDK reads them from environment variables.

77
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:17,000
Your AWS access key, secret key, session token, and region.

78
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Third, if neither of those is set,

79
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
it falls back to your AWS config file

80
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
and the standard credential chain.

81
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:28,000
The practical takeaway is that explicit always beats implicit.

82
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
So if a request authenticates as the wrong identity,

83
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,000
check for stray constructor arguments or environment variables first.

84
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Those credentials can come from three different sources,

85
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
and Anthropic ranks them by preference.

86
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,000
The recommended option is a Bedrock service role,

87
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,000
where the platform assumes the role for you,

88
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
and you manage nothing sensitive directly.

89
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Next are IAM assumed roles,

90
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
which work well for cross-account access

91
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
and cap out at a 12-hour session.

92
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
The least preferred option is a bearer token,

93
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,000
which shares the same 12-hour maximum,

94
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
but is generated separately and passed like an API key.

95
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
A static credential your code now has to handle.

96
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:12,000
All three share one property.

97
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,000
None of them is an Anthropic key.

98
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Your access to Cloud here is entirely governed

99
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,000
by AWS identity and access management,

100
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,000
which is usually exactly what a security team wants.

101
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,000
All right, the gaps.

102
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
A few features you might rely on with the first party API

103
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
simply aren't available on Bedrock,

104
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
and it's better to learn that now than mid-build.

105
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:42,000
First, there is no URL image input and no files API,

106
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:47,000
so any image or document has to be sent inline as base 64,

107
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:50,000
and images are capped at five megabytes each.

108
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Second, the server-side tools don't run here,

109
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,000
so code execution, web search, and web fetch are off the table.

110
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,000
Third, the message batches API and the MCP connector

111
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:04,000
are both unavailable.

112
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,000
None of these block a typical request and response app,

113
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,000
but if your design leaned on batches or server tools,

114
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
you'll need a different plan on Bedrock.

115
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
Let's balance that with the good news,

116
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,000
because plenty carries over untouched.

117
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Prompt caching works, so your repeated context

118
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:24,000
still earns the discount.

119
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Extended thinking works, so reasoning heavy calls behave the same.

120
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
Client-side tool use is fully supported,

121
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:36,000
including the bash, text editor, memory, and computer use tools.

122
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Citations and structured outputs both work as well.

123
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
On top of that, you get a choice of endpoint types.

124
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
Global endpoints carry no premium,

125
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:51,000
while regional endpoints add about 10% for tighter data locality.

126
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
So the honest summary is this.

127
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Design your app to the supported feature set,

128
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:01,000
and a Bedrock deployment behaves almost exactly like a first-party one,

129
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
just with AWS holding the keys.

130
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Let's recap. Bedrock serves Claude through AWS

131
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
with the same messages API,

132
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
but a different client, credentials, and model IDs.

133
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:18,000
Authentication runs on AWS identity, rather than an entropic key,

134
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
with a service role as the recommended default.

135
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:25,000
Caching, thinking, and client tools all carry over,

136
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,000
while batches and server tools don't.

137
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Remember, Bedrock mantle is current, and invoke model is legacy.

138
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
So that's the old way of doing things.

139
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:38,000
That covers invoking Claude on Amazon Bedrock.

140
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:40,000
I'll meet you in the next lesson.

